    "date_str": "2026-07-08",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-07-08",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23939_english_2026-07-08.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-08-2026-07-08-0131",
    "chunk_index": 131,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "8e98bd844bcb999961cd9ed1555b49fc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1963,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, I see an important article by Kamanthi Wickramasinghe in the middle pages - a double-page article - of a newspaper today, particularly on the prison issue and the shortcomings in prisons. Several comments made have been reported in that and particularly those by Ambika Satkunanathan, former Commissioner of the Human Rights Commission, have brought to light very, very important - salient - issues, which must be discussed.\n\nAnd, we have to bring in more reforms to resolve these problems, particularly which relate to those who cannot pay fines. Almost 60 per cent of the prisoners are either drug offenders or people who cannot pay fines. Those are very small fines that have to be paid. That is what you call punishing people who are in poverty and it is a very serious issue. So, as a humanitarian issue, the Government can create certain funds for, at least, those who cannot afford to pay the fines. Then, those who cannot pay the fines could be released with that very simple solution. Together with that, the drug offenders must be treated as patients. They could be provided with rehabilitation facilities to rehabilitate them from their addiction, not prisons. That is an important thing. So, several such issues will have to be addressed and all these problems will have to be solved.\n\nIn the meantime, this racist attitude in certain prisons, where Muslim prisoners have been prevented from using\n\ntheir prayer rooms for daily five-time prayers, must be dismissed and that practice should be restored immediately with action and the mosque in Mahara should also be restored to its original position. That position taken by the authorities of the Mahara Prison must be dismissed because that is a very racist attitude. That is an incident where this Government, as it always claims to be non-racist, should not believe what the officials tell you, but instead look at what really is happening on the ground and solve the problem for those people."
  },
