    "chunk_text": "\u2022 Accordingly, the supervisory framework adopted by the BSD is focused on prudential supervision and should not be construed as a financial audit, investigative exercise or forensic audit.\n\n\u2022 This process supported to ensure the\n\n3. \u2022 The investigations by the law enforcement\n\nauthorities and other relevant parties are ongoing.\n\n\u2022 Bank Supervision Department examination\n\nup to this point reveals that the total value involved in this incident of NDB is Rs. 13.2 billion.\n\n\u2022 No losses have come to light in any other\n\nfinancial institution.\n\n4. The payment infrastructure provider, that is LankaPay (Pvt.) Ltd., normally called \u201cLankaPay\u201d, facilitates the switching of transaction between the originating institution and the receiving institution and operates as a \"pass-through.\" Currently, LankaPay neither has the authority nor the mandate to verify the authenticity of the transactions processed through its systems nor to validate or check the legitimacy of transactions.\n\n5. \u2022 The proposed dividends amounting to Rs. 2.76\n\nbillion scheduled to be paid on 06th April, 2026 were suspended by an order of BSD, in terms Section 45(1) of the Banking Act, No. 30 of 1988, as amended.\n\n635 636\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dd4\u0dc0\u0dc3\u0d85\u0d86\u0da0\u0daf\u0d9a\u0d9a\"\u0dc3\u0dc4\u0dba\u0dbb\u0dd2\u0dba\u0dc3\u0dad\u0dcf \u0dc3\u0dd6\u0dba\u0dbb\u0d9a]\n\n2026 \u0da2\u0dd6\u0dd2\u0dc350\u0dc3\n\n\u2022 The total value of cash dividends withheld for\n\nall institutional shareholders is Rs. 2.19 billion, of which EPF was entitled to receive approximately Rs. 260 million.\n\n6. Financial Institutions in Sri Lanka such as the National Development Bank are liable for the following taxes:\n\n\u2022 Corporate Income Tax - CIT - of 30 per cent;\n\n\u2022 Value Added Tax -VAT- of 18 per cent and\n\nSocial Security Contribution Levy - SSCL - of 2.5 per cent on financial services;\n\n\u2022 A final Withholding Tax - WHT - on Advance\n\nIncome Tax - AIT - of 15 per cent on dividend. That is, as a final tax.\n\nHence, the tax implications of the financial fraud of Rs. 13.2 billion will depend upon the accounting treatment of the resultant loss due to the fraud. That is yet to be done."
