    "md5": "3067602de1a23704223ad72ea89160c7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1725,
    "chunk_text": "Income Tax - AIT - of 15 per cent on dividend. That is, as a final tax.\n\nHence, the tax implications of the financial fraud of Rs. 13.2 billion will depend upon the accounting treatment of the resultant loss due to the fraud. That is yet to be done.\n\nAccordingly, the related fiscal impact cannot be reliably estimated until the forensic review is completed and the final accounting treatment is determined.\n\nAs per the information gathered from authorized representative of the Bank, the forensic review started by the Central Bank of Sri Lanka has not yet been finalized and the final quantified impact of the fraud remains subject to confirmation.\n\nIn view of the above circumstances, particularly:\n\n\u2022 the absence of finalized forensic findings by\n\nthe Central Bank of Sri Lanka,\n\n\u2022 the non- submission of amended tax return,\n\nreflecting the reported fraud losses along with restated financial statements by the Bank, the Inland Revenue Department is presently not in a position to reliably estimate the total fiscal impact, including direct tax losses, indirect tax losses and any potential non-collectability arising from the reported fraud incident.\n\n7. As mentioned in answer to Question No. 3 above, the relevant investigations in this regard are currently ongoing.\n\n8. \u2022 NDB\u2019s share price followed a downward trend\n\nsince the first market disclosure of information on 02nd April, 2026, starting at Rs. 130.50 and declined to Rs. 116.00 by 04th May, 2026.\n\n\u2022 NDB\u2019s market capitalization was approximately Rs. 56 billion at the time of disclosure of information and since then, the share price has been between Rs. 111 and Rs. 116 and hence, market capitalization has come down to Rs. 48 to Rs. 50 billion."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-10-2026-07-10",
    "num": "2026-07-10",
    "date_str": "2026-07-10",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-07-10",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23970_english_2026-07-10.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-10-2026-07-10-0079",
    "chunk_index": 79,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "afec9ed2607f2861dd4d218676db7811",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1495,
    "chunk_text": "\u2022 NDB\u2019s market capitalization was approximately Rs. 56 billion at the time of disclosure of information and since then, the share price has been between Rs. 111 and Rs. 116 and hence, market capitalization has come down to Rs. 48 to Rs. 50 billion.\n\nThank you.\n\n\u0d9c\u0dd4 \u0d9a\u0dae\u0dcf\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dba\u0d9a\u0dd4\u0dbb\u0dd9\u0dcf (\u0bc7\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bc7\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0b9a\u0baa\u0bbe\u0ba8\u0bbe\u0baf\u0b95\u0bb0\u0bcd \u0b85\u0bb5\u0bb0\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bb3\u0bcd) (The Hon. Speaker) Hon. Ravi Karunanayake, you can get a short clarification.\n\n\u0d9c\u0dd4 \u0dbb\u0dd3 \u0d9a\u0dd4\u0dab\u0dcf\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dba\u0d9a \u0dd9\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf (\u0bc7\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bc7\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0bb0\u0bb5\u0bbf \u0b95\u0bb0\u0bc1\u0ba3\u0bbe\u0ba8\u0bbe\u0baf\u0b95\u0bcd\u0b95) (The Hon. Ravi Karunanayake) Hon. Speaker, I think the quality of the answers that come from the Central Bank of Sri Lanka is deteriorating by the day. With regard to this information that comes in, Hon. Minister, you have now become only a post box and nothing more than that. I am sorry to say that, but we are playing around with the financial system of this country. Sir, firstly, the information that has been given is wrong. While this Question is being answered today, Deloitte has given an internal report saying that the loss is Rs. 13.5 billion, whereas the Central Bank is reporting through the Minister that it is Rs. 13. 2 billion.\n\nSir, by asking these questions in Parliament, if we do not get the truth coming out from them, from what institution could we get information on the reality? This is not the first time this is happening, Hon. Minister. So, what you reported was wrong."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-10-2026-07-10",
    "num": "2026-07-10",
    "date_str": "2026-07-10",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-07-10",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/23970_english_2026-07-10.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-10-2026-07-10-0080",
    "chunk_index": 80,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "00d06d02ae4d628a6fbfd6847df5925f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2144,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, by asking these questions in Parliament, if we do not get the truth coming out from them, from what institution could we get information on the reality? This is not the first time this is happening, Hon. Minister. So, what you reported was wrong.\n\nThe most important thing I want to highlight is this. In answer to question number 2, the Hon. Minister was saying that suspicious transactions must be reported to the FIU. That is the accepted norm. My question was\n\n637 638\n\n\u0db4\u0daf\u0d9a\u0dd2\u0dda\u0dca\u0dca \u0dad\u0dd4\u0db8\u0dc0\u0dca\u200d\n\nregarding that. He was telling me the same thing that I asked from him. We brought the officials responsible before the Committee on Public Finance and we did not get an answer. I am sorry, but there is a deterioration, a degeneration. If this type of thing goes on, it will create a huge problem.\n\nNot only that, Hon. Minister, -\n\n\u0d9c\u0dd4 \u0d9a\u0dae\u0dcf\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dba\u0d9a\u0dd4\u0dbb\u0dd9\u0dcf (\u0bc7\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bc7\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0b9a\u0baa\u0bbe\u0ba8\u0bbe\u0baf\u0b95\u0bb0\u0bcd \u0b85\u0bb5\u0bb0\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bb3\u0bcd) (The Hon. Speaker) Hon. Member, you have to shorten your explanation and ask the question.\n\n\u0d9c\u0dd4 \u0dbb\u0dd3 \u0d9a\u0dd4\u0dab\u0dcf\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dba\u0d9a \u0dd9\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf (\u0bc7\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bc7\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0bb0\u0bb5\u0bbf \u0b95\u0bb0\u0bc1\u0ba3\u0bbe\u0ba8\u0bbe\u0baf\u0b95\u0bcd\u0b95) (The Hon. Ravi Karunanayake) I am making an explanation because it is very important, Sir, and let me ask the question.\n\nHon. Minister, there is another thing. A suspicious transaction has two ends. If one bank has a problem, it is remitted to another bank. There are six banks and 10 non-banking institutions. Two banks have reported it regarding the NDB. So, on this score, if there are any suspicious transactions, the other bank has a right to inform about it to the FIU. So, are you telling me that 3,000 transactions could take place without the Bank Supervision Department finding the trend in the movement of those funds? If so, I think we are in serious jeopardy. So, Sir, I would greatly appreciate if you would, through the Hon. Minister, get this verified. If you want, you could retract this Question and get them to answer it properly because I am appalled at the level of reporting by such an independent institution to Parliament."
  },
