    "chunk_text": "(v) the livelihood opportunities currently being implemented and planned to be initiated in the future, including training of local people in the fields of eco-tourism guidance, boat services and accommodation facilities?\n\n(b) If not, why?\n\n(a) (i) Yes. The Kokkilai and Nayaru lagoons in the Mullaitivu District, the wetlands and bird habitats near them have been identified as areas with a very high potential for development of eco-tourism. The Tourism Development Plan 2026-2030 prepared by the Northern Provincial Tourism Bureau has also identified those as major tourist attractions. Also, the natural ecosystems in these areas provide habitats for numerous native - endemic - inhabitant and migratory bird species, and it has been identified that there is great potential for nature-based tourism activities such as bird watching, lagoon tours, environmental studies, photography and guided ecological tours supported by its rich biodiversity and scenic natural landscapes. (ii) Considering the high environmental value\n\nof Kokkilai and Nayaru lagoons as well as their associated wetlands and bird habitats, there is significant potential for development of eco-hotels. However, at present, eco-hotel facilities are very limited in the Kokkilai and Nayaru areas of the Mullaitivu District and the existing facilities are mainly, small-scale guest houses and basic accommodation facilities, which are insufficient to meet the growing demand for nature and environmental tourism needs. (iii) Yes. The Northern Provincial Tourism Bureau has prepared the 2026-2030 Tourism Development Plan with the support of the Faculty of Postgraduate Studies of the University of Vavuniya and under the leadership of district-level stakeholders, through which the tourism potential, the major tourist attractions, resources,\n\n987 988\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0dad\u0dd4 \u0dbb\u0dbb\u0dcf\u0dc3\u0dcf \u0dbb\u0dd2\u0dc3\u0d9a\u0dbb\u0db1\u0dca \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf]\n\n2026 \u0dd6\u0dbd\u0dd2 00"
  },
