    "chunk_text": "It is important to note, however, that while the underlying land within the Area of Authority of the Colombo Port City is made available exclusively on a leasehold basis, the Colombo Port City Economic Commission Act expressly contemplates the creation and disposition of condominium parcels. In terms of Sections 38(c) and 38(d) of the Colombo Port City Economic Commission Act, No. 11 of 2021, the Commission may, jointly with the relevant investor, developer or Project Company, lease or\n\n1181 1182\n\n2026 \u0da2\u0dd6\u0dbd\u0dd2 23\n\nFurther, Sections 6(1)(e) and 6(1)(f) of the Colombo Port City Economic Commission Act empower the Commission to lease or transfer on a freehold basis condominium parcels on Government and Project Company Marketable Land. These provisions, read together with Sections 38(c) and 38(d), demonstrate that the Act distinguishes between ownership of the underlying land and the ownership or transfer of condominium parcels constructed thereon.\n\nAccordingly, where condominium parcels have been lawfully transferred to third parties in accordance with the Colombo Port City Economic Commission Act and the applicable transaction documents, such interests would be governed by the terms of the relevant transfer instruments and applicable law.\n\n(e) The answer is provided for in the Indenture of Lease entered between the Colombo Port City Economic Commission and the lessee under lessee's obligations.\n\n(f) There are no legal provisions for automatic renewal or preferential automatic renewal. The lease shall be valid only for a period of 99 years.\n\nUpon the natural expiry of the lease, as provided for in the Indenture of Lease, the Development Site is to be handed back to the Colombo Port City Economic Commission and the Commission is not obliged to compensate the lessee for any improvements or developments remaining on the site, and the lessee does not require the removal of buildings upon expiry.\n\nProvision 2.6 (d) of the Indenture of Lease provides as follows:"
