    "md5": "fdb4920122843d42bd059e9c34cbfafa",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1316,
    "chunk_text": "Madam, no person is born a racist; no person is born hating another person\u2019s race or religion. Hatred is taught; it is learnt. People are born with natural feelings like love, compassion and brotherhood. Those are the feelings that are natural to a human being. So, it is with those feelings and unity that we survived for thousands of years; that is how we survived. And, hatred, racial hatred especially, is being taught for political reasons.\n\nI remember that the Tamil people who lived among us lived with no fear. Our cultural identity, our friendship and all were based on that. As a child, I remember that the first sweet I tasted was \"sookiri\" when I was in Jaffna. That is how I remember Jaffna. The first time I ate \"idli\" in my life was in Kandy at Uncle Ganesan\u2019s house while we were watching the Kandy Perahera. That is how we were raised and that is what we remember.\n\n1297 1298\n\n[\u0dba\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0dc4\u0dd4\u0da2\u0dd2\u0db6\u0dd4\u0dbb\u0dca \u0dcf\u0dab \u0dcf\u0db1\u0dca\u0daf \u0dc4\u0dd2\u0dcf]\n\n2026 \u0da2\u0dd6\u0dbd\u0dd2 23\n\nIn July, 1983, in my house, we had to keep Uncle Ganesan, his wife and his family. When they were seing other places being burnt down, they were in tears inside our rooms, fearing for their lives. Remember, the Sinhala people or the Sri Lankan Colombo people stood by the Tamil people, but the State-sponsored terrorism went on."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
