    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "ad7d2b399f40fb41be766d4eabc580ea",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1473,
    "chunk_text": "In July, 1983, in my house, we had to keep Uncle Ganesan, his wife and his family. When they were seing other places being burnt down, they were in tears inside our rooms, fearing for their lives. Remember, the Sinhala people or the Sri Lankan Colombo people stood by the Tamil people, but the State-sponsored terrorism went on.\n\nMy father, being a filmmaker, always believed that we must have a Tamil film industry. So, many years later, he made a movie called \u201cAnuragam\u201d and another called \u201cNadu Potra Vaalga\u201d, with which Uncle Ganeshan was involved, both as a producer as well as an actor. It was only much later, in the 1990s, that I realized that Uncle Ganeshan and his wife, who lived with his family in our house, were actually the Hon. Mano Ganesan\u2019s parents. I did not know that earlier; only in the late 1990s I realized that and I spoke to him. So, that is how we looked after each other. At the same time, there was also a great filmmaker called Venkat. We called him Uncle Venkat. He was the director of \u201cSiri Pathula\u201d, a movie about the Buddha. My father told him - we were living at No. 15, Peiris Road - \u201cVenkat, do not go out today. Today seems to be a little unsettling. So, you had better stay with us tonight.\u201d He said, \u201cThis is my village. Why should I fear? I am the man who made that movie about the Buddha. So, who would touch me?\u201d Only fifteen minutes later, we got the message that he was burned alive inside his own vehicle."
  },
