    "md5": "f1fe4cd69892f401b00b3a6228f8fa78",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1798,
    "chunk_text": "My father, being a filmmaker, always believed that we must have a Tamil film industry. So, many years later, he made a movie called \u201cAnuragam\u201d and another called \u201cNadu Potra Vaalga\u201d, with which Uncle Ganeshan was involved, both as a producer as well as an actor. It was only much later, in the 1990s, that I realized that Uncle Ganeshan and his wife, who lived with his family in our house, were actually the Hon. Mano Ganesan\u2019s parents. I did not know that earlier; only in the late 1990s I realized that and I spoke to him. So, that is how we looked after each other. At the same time, there was also a great filmmaker called Venkat. We called him Uncle Venkat. He was the director of \u201cSiri Pathula\u201d, a movie about the Buddha. My father told him - we were living at No. 15, Peiris Road - \u201cVenkat, do not go out today. Today seems to be a little unsettling. So, you had better stay with us tonight.\u201d He said, \u201cThis is my village. Why should I fear? I am the man who made that movie about the Buddha. So, who would touch me?\u201d Only fifteen minutes later, we got the message that he was burned alive inside his own vehicle.\n\nIsland, we want to find out and bring a closure to those cases. So, as the Minister of Justice and National Integration, I am glad that I spoke today, sharing my personal experiences. As a child, I saw when I was in Kalubowila Junction how they came and killed the man who had been mending our shoes for years in a brutal manner. I had seen the people wounded and who were trying to reach the hospital being beaten up by the racist goons. I feel embarrassed because, now, I meet so many of my friends who are living overseas. After the \u201883 riots, they left this country and they are not here. But, they have forgiven us; those people have forgiven us."
