    "description": "Hansard of 2026-07-24",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24106_english_2026-07-24.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-24-2026-07-24-0107",
    "chunk_index": 107,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "1450cae05d0728714c7054340215181c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1431,
    "chunk_text": "Then, what else has been done? Much has been said about the inadequacies in the Department of Government Analyst. Yes, we agree and we know that. That is why the vacancies in the Department of Government Analyst have been filled; that is why money has been allocated for the purchasing of the much-needed equipment, which had not been purchased until two years ago. The procurement process has started for all of those things, none of which have happened earlier. Also, the procedures and processes in the Department of Government Analyst are being reviewed and revised and interventions have been made to speed up its work. So, that is what the Hon. Minister has been doing for the last two years. And, yes, the Department of Government Analyst is heavily under-resourced and understaffed. But, that did not happen during the last two years. That was the Department of Government Analyst this Government inherited and the Hon. Minister had to take on. Let us not forget that.\n\nLet me remind this House that at the time when this Government came into power and the Hon. Minister took office, there had already been 31,000-odd prisoners incarcerated. So, that is not something that has happened over the last two years; that has been a growing problem, which had gone unaddressed and which this Government and this Minister has been addressing. So, Sir, those are the facts that we need to consider when we are debating this Motion."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
