    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24106_english_2026-07-24.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-24-2026-07-24-0108",
    "chunk_index": 108,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "54a7d3e9ba796d2dfcf709989bfec91d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1660,
    "chunk_text": "Let me remind this House that at the time when this Government came into power and the Hon. Minister took office, there had already been 31,000-odd prisoners incarcerated. So, that is not something that has happened over the last two years; that has been a growing problem, which had gone unaddressed and which this Government and this Minister has been addressing. So, Sir, those are the facts that we need to consider when we are debating this Motion.\n\nAlso, Sir, since this is a Motion that has been brought against an individual, a Member of the Cabinet, we need to look at what this Minister has done as opposed to the conduct of previous Minsters in charge of this subject as well. There also needs to be that comparison because this is a Motion brought against an individual. Sir, we know that this is not the first instance this kind of an incident took place in our prison system. It is tragic, but that is the truth that we need to acknowledge.\n\nIf you remember, this is the month that marks a darkest part of our history; this is the month in which our country spiralled into a bout of ethnic violence that lasted for so many years. Let us not forget what happened in the Welikada Prison in 1983, where hundreds of prisoners were killed as part of the violence that took place in the Island at that time. Also, in 2012, a similar protest took place, where 20 prisoners and 13 STF officers were killed. Then, in 2020, in the Mahara Prison, 11 inmates were killed. At no point did any of those Ministers of Justice at that time resign. So, what is the difference that the Opposition is demanding today with the resignation of this Minister of Justice?"
  },
