    "chunk_text": "If you remember, this is the month that marks a darkest part of our history; this is the month in which our country spiralled into a bout of ethnic violence that lasted for so many years. Let us not forget what happened in the Welikada Prison in 1983, where hundreds of prisoners were killed as part of the violence that took place in the Island at that time. Also, in 2012, a similar protest took place, where 20 prisoners and 13 STF officers were killed. Then, in 2020, in the Mahara Prison, 11 inmates were killed. At no point did any of those Ministers of Justice at that time resign. So, what is the difference that the Opposition is demanding today with the resignation of this Minister of Justice?\n\n1383 1384\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0db8\u0dda\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\norganizing of criminal activities took place outside, a similar amount, if not a greater amount, of it was going on within the prison system, led and managed by those in the prison itself. Mobile phones were widely available and all kinds of things were going on within the prison system with the involvement of a few prison officers and, as was established now, with those even more powerful.\n\nSo, Sir, this is the corrupt system that this Minister of Justice has been tasked with cleaning up, and that involves investigations within the institution itself and transferring, investigation and prosecution of his own officials! Therefore, it is not an easy task, but he has begun that task. This Government has undertaken that extremely difficult challenge of cleaning up within, cleaning up the institutions. We inherited institutions which were destroyed, corrupt and where corruption was the reason for the existence of many of those institutions. We also know of a Deputy Minister of Justice who went into a prison drunk and threatened prisoners just because he could! That was the culture that existed and that is the culture this Minister of Justice is trying to reform, trying to change."
  },
