    "num": "2026-07-24",
    "date_str": "2026-07-24",
    "description": "Hansard of 2026-07-24",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24106_english_2026-07-24.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-24-2026-07-24-0161",
    "chunk_index": 161,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "ce3aab4487785c2b3e74740f5e41426a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1883,
    "chunk_text": "Hon. Deputy Chairperson of Committees, I would also like to comment on one other matter raised in this House today about the independence of the Judiciary. That was raised by several Members of the Opposition, especially by the Hon. Rauff Hakeem. I would like to comment on it because it is being misinterpreted. What is the independence of the Judiciary has to be understood before we comment on it or politicize it.\n\nAnd, Madam, this particular allegation that we are compromising the independence of the Judiciary is baseless. Since we came into power, we have appointed several Chief Justices based only on superiority. You can\n\ncheck on that; any Member of Parliament can check on that. We have not appointed any outsiders to the Judiciary, to the Court of Appeal or to the Supreme Court. We have only promoted judges who are in the Court of Appeal and the Supreme Court to the positions of Chairman of the Court of Appeal and Chief Justice. So, we have not interfered with the Judiciary at any point. However, we should remember, under every President before President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, there have been such appointments made, especially to the Chief Justice position. Especially to the position of Chief Justice, every single President had appointed outsiders. That is the practice of interfering with the Judiciary. You all appointed your loyalists and people you preferred to the Judiciary, but we have not done that. All of these judges who are purported to get extension of retirement age are currently in the Judiciary. They are in the Magistrate\u2019s Court, the District Court, the High Court, the Court of Appeal and the Supreme Court. We are not making fresh appointments to those positions, except for the recruitment process that has been going on as Magistrates. Therefore, this allegation that we are making interference with the judicial power is baseless."
  },
